1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,363
An auditor asks that question, and
most teams answer with a name.

2
00:00:08,403 --> 00:00:13,286
A branching model is a team's agreement
about its branches: its names for lines of

3
00:00:13,326 --> 00:00:15,807
commits, the saved snapshots of a project.

4
00:00:16,147 --> 00:00:21,068
The name tells the auditor nothing: not how
long branches live, not which way fixes

5
00:00:21,108 --> 00:00:25,970
travel, not who may move which ref, meaning
a branch or a tag, not what was in the

6
00:00:26,010 --> 00:00:30,691
last patch release. The chapter's advice
is blunt: don't adopt a model by name.

7
00:00:30,731 --> 00:00:33,859
Write down the refs, the
rules and the fix direction.

8
00:00:34,199 --> 00:00:38,784
Level 8 ends by asking you to do exactly
that for a whole company, and then to

9
00:00:38,824 --> 00:00:42,578
defend it against five people who
each want something different.

10
00:00:42,618 --> 00:00:44,949
So what do you tell the auditor instead?

11
00:00:46,789 --> 00:00:50,532
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:50,572 --> 00:00:55,617
Every earlier level ended with a gate. This
one ends with a design review, because

13
00:00:55,657 --> 00:01:00,082
the skill of Level 8 can't be checked
by a repository in a broken state.

14
00:01:00,122 --> 00:01:04,547
You're given a company and its
repository, the project's stored history.

15
00:01:04,587 --> 00:01:09,570
You measure it, design the company's
branching model, governance, CI and security

16
00:01:09,610 --> 00:01:13,539
policy, write the design down,
and defend it against objections.

17
00:01:13,579 --> 00:01:16,081
CI means automated checks on each change.

18
00:01:16,421 --> 00:01:21,099
This briefing tells you what is asked,
how it's judged, how the evidence is

19
00:01:21,139 --> 00:01:25,251
gathered, and how to structure a
design. It shows no model design.

20
00:01:25,291 --> 00:01:29,277
There's none: the lab file says
so, and so does its answer file.

21
00:01:29,317 --> 00:01:34,498
A design is judged against a rubric, a
scoring sheet, and two different designs can

22
00:01:34,538 --> 00:01:35,127
both pass.

23
00:01:36,968 --> 00:01:38,380
After this video you can:

24
00:01:38,720 --> 00:01:41,701
State what the design review
asks for and how it is judged.

25
00:01:42,041 --> 00:01:46,436
Collect the evidence a design needs from
an existing repository with read-only

26
00:01:46,476 --> 00:01:46,947
commands.

27
00:01:47,287 --> 00:01:52,181
Structure a design as decisions, each with
its reason, its cost and the control that

28
00:01:52,221 --> 00:01:52,886
enforces it.

29
00:01:53,226 --> 00:01:57,136
Defend a design against objections
without retreating to "best practice".

30
00:01:58,976 --> 00:02:04,541
What is asked. A design document of at
most four pages, plus an evidence table.

31
00:02:04,581 --> 00:02:09,721
It has seven parts, in this order.
Evidence. Branching and release model.

32
00:02:09,761 --> 00:02:14,972
Governance. CI design. Security policy.
Reproducibility of model releases.

33
00:02:15,012 --> 00:02:15,965
And migration.

34
00:02:16,305 --> 00:02:21,316
Two requirements run through all seven. For
every control, you state the layer that

35
00:02:21,356 --> 00:02:26,427
enforces it: a convention, a local hook, a
CI check, a GitHub rule, or a contractual

36
00:02:26,467 --> 00:02:31,295
or human process. And for every factual
claim about GitHub, whether a feature, a

37
00:02:31,335 --> 00:02:36,407
plan requirement or a default, you cite the
chapter of the book or the documentation

38
00:02:36,447 --> 00:02:41,336
page. If you don't know whether a plan
includes a feature, you say so and say how

39
00:02:41,376 --> 00:02:42,492
you would find out.

40
00:02:42,832 --> 00:02:48,186
How it is judged. One hundred points, pass
at 70 or more, with no automatic failure.

41
00:02:48,526 --> 00:02:53,430
The automatic failures. Five, whatever the
points. Read them as the five things this

42
00:02:53,470 --> 00:02:55,784
part of the course taught you not to do.

43
00:02:56,124 --> 00:03:01,030
Claiming that research proves one branching
strategy is best, or citing a number for

44
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:01,207
it.

45
00:03:01,547 --> 00:03:06,257
A policy whose only enforcement is a local
hook or a convention, presented as a

46
00:03:06,297 --> 00:03:06,738
control.

47
00:03:07,078 --> 00:03:11,692
Treating a commit that deletes a secret,
a force push, or making a repository

48
00:03:11,732 --> 00:03:13,686
private as removal of the secret.

49
00:03:14,026 --> 00:03:18,817
Inventing a GitHub feature, default or plan
requirement, or a Git command or option.

50
00:03:19,157 --> 00:03:23,609
A design that cannot produce a patch release
for a customer containing only fixes.

51
00:03:23,949 --> 00:03:29,092
Quick quiz. A policy is enforced only by
a local hook, a script Git runs on one

52
00:03:29,132 --> 00:03:33,816
person's own machine. A, a finished
control, or B, an automatic failure?

53
00:03:36,156 --> 00:03:41,387
B, the second on that list. A hook isn't
cloned, so the person it controls can step

54
00:03:41,427 --> 00:03:41,895
over it.

55
00:03:59,735 --> 00:04:04,951
The defence. After you submit, the assessor
raises objections, one at a time, from

56
00:04:04,991 --> 00:04:08,733
five directions. A developer
who finds the model too heavy.

57
00:04:08,773 --> 00:04:12,194
A release manager with an
urgent fix for one customer.

58
00:04:12,234 --> 00:04:17,514
A security reviewer. An auditor. And the
CTO, asking about cost and about evidence.

59
00:04:17,854 --> 00:04:21,868
For each objection you may defend
the design, amend it, or concede.

60
00:04:21,908 --> 00:04:25,497
A reasoned amendment scores as
well as a successful defence.

61
00:04:25,537 --> 00:04:30,458
What costs points is changing the design
without saying what the change breaks, or

62
00:04:30,498 --> 00:04:33,423
defending a control you
can't say how to enforce.

63
00:04:33,763 --> 00:04:38,650
How to structure a decision. Each decision
in your design has the same four lines.

64
00:04:38,690 --> 00:04:43,336
The decision. The reason, which is a
measurement you made, a mechanism you can

65
00:04:43,376 --> 00:04:47,903
explain, or a source you can cite. The
cost. And the layer that enforces it.

66
00:04:47,943 --> 00:04:52,710
A decision whose "enforced by" line reads
"people will remember" isn't finished.

67
00:04:53,050 --> 00:04:58,181
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds. Write
those four lines for one rule you would

68
00:04:58,221 --> 00:05:01,546
want on a shared project.
Read the last line out loud.

69
00:05:06,386 --> 00:05:11,239
A layer, such as a CI check, can be tested.
"People will remember" can't, so that's

70
00:05:11,279 --> 00:05:12,418
the line to work on.

71
00:05:14,258 --> 00:05:19,064
Think of the design as a set of load-bearing
claims, like the members of a bridge.

72
00:05:19,104 --> 00:05:23,792
Each claim carries something: a patch
release that contains only fixes, a secret

73
00:05:23,832 --> 00:05:27,456
that can't arrive, a model that
can be named five years later.

74
00:05:27,496 --> 00:05:31,001
An objection in the defence is
a load applied to one member.

75
00:05:31,041 --> 00:05:34,547
If the member has a reason and
an enforcing layer, it holds.

76
00:05:34,587 --> 00:05:38,625
If it rests on "that is best practice",
it has nothing underneath it.

77
00:05:38,965 --> 00:05:43,496
Where the picture breaks: a bridge member
that fails is a defect, and in the defence

78
00:05:43,536 --> 00:05:48,067
a member you replace isn't. You're allowed,
and scored well, for amending a decision

79
00:05:48,107 --> 00:05:52,257
under a good objection, provided you say
what the amendment breaks elsewhere.

80
00:05:52,597 --> 00:05:56,054
The second model is the one
from video 177: the layers.

81
00:05:56,094 --> 00:06:00,061
A habit, a client setting, a
server rule, a review, a pipeline.

82
00:06:00,101 --> 00:06:05,403
For every control in your design, be able
to point at its layer and say who can step

83
00:06:05,443 --> 00:06:08,329
over it and how that
would be seen afterwards.

84
00:06:10,169 --> 00:06:13,262
The sheet is the skeleton
of one decision per block.

85
00:06:13,302 --> 00:06:17,902
A real design has several decisions per
block, and the lab's deliverable adds

86
00:06:17,942 --> 00:06:22,904
reproducibility and migration. Use the sheet
to test each decision before you write

87
00:06:22,944 --> 00:06:26,098
prose around it. The blocks
stay empty in this video.

88
00:06:27,938 --> 00:06:32,651
Step 1: which branches exist, how
old are they, how far from main?

89
00:06:32,991 --> 00:06:37,518
Before the output: what would you expect
this to look like for a team that deploys

90
00:06:37,558 --> 00:06:38,687
several times a week?

91
00:06:39,027 --> 00:06:44,018
Three columns per branch: the date of its
last commit, ahead and behind relative to

92
00:06:44,058 --> 00:06:48,867
main, the name. Ahead and behind count
commits one side has and the other lacks.

93
00:06:48,907 --> 00:06:53,837
This is a measurement of branch lifetime,
the variable that video 173 said you can

94
00:06:53,877 --> 00:06:54,261
act on.

95
00:06:54,601 --> 00:06:57,154
Step 2: what was released, and from where?

96
00:06:57,494 --> 00:07:01,988
The tags, names that aren't expected to
move, with their dates and the commits they

97
00:07:02,028 --> 00:07:04,774
name, and a graph reduced
to the decorated commits.

98
00:07:04,814 --> 00:07:07,669
Look at where each tag sits
relative to the branches.

99
00:07:08,009 --> 00:07:11,799
Step 3: is there a fix on a release
branch that main never received?

100
00:07:12,139 --> 00:07:18,045
Predict, from video 172, what an empty
result means and what a printed line means.

101
00:07:18,085 --> 00:07:19,205
Say it out loud.

102
00:07:24,045 --> 00:07:28,652
The command lists commits on the release
branch with no equivalent change on main.

103
00:07:28,692 --> 00:07:32,449
Empty means none. One command prints
a line and one prints nothing.

104
00:07:32,489 --> 00:07:36,982
Remember also the limit of this check,
because the rubric asks for the limits of

105
00:07:37,022 --> 00:07:37,945
each measurement.

106
00:07:38,285 --> 00:07:40,831
Step 4: how does work reach main?

107
00:07:41,171 --> 00:07:45,240
Merge commits, which have more than
one parent, against ordinary commits.

108
00:07:45,280 --> 00:07:49,686
Then the first-parent log with dates, and
the distance and the age of the split

109
00:07:49,726 --> 00:07:51,093
between main and develop.

110
00:07:51,433 --> 00:07:54,450
Step 5: separate finished
work from open work.

111
00:07:54,790 --> 00:07:59,071
This is the content check of video
174 applied to every unmerged branch.

112
00:07:59,111 --> 00:08:03,692
It exists because the commit-based checks
mislead after a squash merge, which

113
00:08:03,732 --> 00:08:08,313
combines several commits into one new
commit. In the lab you meet that as the

114
00:08:08,353 --> 00:08:12,214
failure scenario, and you correct
your evidence table afterwards.

115
00:08:12,554 --> 00:08:17,514
Stop here. Numbers, and no conclusion.
Whose work is the conclusion?

116
00:08:19,854 --> 00:08:24,661
Yours. The replay contains more measurements:
the content of the history, people and

117
00:08:24,701 --> 00:08:29,394
messages. You run those yourself. And no
conclusion is drawn on screen: what these

118
00:08:29,434 --> 00:08:34,241
numbers mean for the company in the brief
is the first fifteen points of the review.

119
00:08:36,081 --> 00:08:40,892
Starting with the name of a model. Root
cause: a name states neither the refs, nor

120
00:08:40,932 --> 00:08:45,210
the rules, nor the fix direction, so
nothing in the design can be tested.

121
00:08:45,550 --> 00:08:48,586
Asserting facts about the
repository without a command.

122
00:08:48,626 --> 00:08:53,114
Root cause: the evidence part is scored
on measurements tied to statements of the

123
00:08:53,154 --> 00:08:55,519
brief, and an impression
cannot be checked.

124
00:08:55,859 --> 00:09:00,623
Presenting a hook or a convention as a
control. Root cause: the enforcing layer is

125
00:09:00,663 --> 00:09:05,311
one the controlled person can step over,
which the rubric treats as an automatic

126
00:09:05,351 --> 00:09:05,779
failure.

127
00:09:06,119 --> 00:09:10,094
Amending the design under pressure
without stating the consequence.

128
00:09:10,134 --> 00:09:14,768
Root cause: each decision carries a load
elsewhere in the design, and a silent

129
00:09:14,808 --> 00:09:16,925
change leaves that load unsupported.

130
00:09:17,265 --> 00:09:20,459
Filling a gap with an invented
feature or plan requirement.

131
00:09:20,499 --> 00:09:25,010
Root cause: the claim was not checked against
the book or the documentation, and "I

132
00:09:25,050 --> 00:09:29,341
do not know, and here is how I would find
out" was available and scores better.

133
00:09:31,181 --> 00:09:35,828
Now, out of the lab. A staff engineer is
asked to propose a new workflow for a

134
00:09:35,868 --> 00:09:40,215
company that runs a hosted product and
also ships an on-premises edition.

135
00:09:40,255 --> 00:09:45,143
In the review meeting a developer objects
that release branches are heavy and that

136
00:09:45,183 --> 00:09:47,667
the team should tag main and nothing more.

137
00:09:48,007 --> 00:09:51,551
She doesn't answer with "release
branches are best practice".

138
00:09:51,591 --> 00:09:56,486
She answers with a measurement and a mechanism:
the on-premises customers take patch

139
00:09:56,526 --> 00:10:01,421
releases only, and under tags on main the
range between two tags contains everything

140
00:10:01,461 --> 00:10:06,062
merged since, which she shows with one
git log between the last two tags of the

141
00:10:06,102 --> 00:10:07,237
existing repository.

142
00:10:07,577 --> 00:10:12,220
Then she states the cost of her own proposal,
a pipeline per supported line and a

143
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:16,730
port check as a release gate, and offers
the amendment she can accept: release

144
00:10:16,770 --> 00:10:21,471
branches only for the on-premises minors,
cut late, and nothing downstream of main

145
00:10:21,511 --> 00:10:26,270
for the hosted product. The developer's
objection changed the design, the change is

146
00:10:26,310 --> 00:10:31,011
written down with what it affects, and the
decision now has a reason that survives

147
00:10:31,051 --> 00:10:32,110
the next objection.

148
00:10:33,950 --> 00:10:37,832
Your turn. Do Lab 34.1,
"The design review", in

149
00:10:37,872 --> 00:10:44,758
lab-manual/m34-practices-design-review.md.
Read the brief twice before you open the

150
00:10:44,798 --> 00:10:45,592
lab shell.

151
00:10:45,932 --> 00:10:50,501
Before you run any command, write down for
each of the four reported problems of the

152
00:10:50,541 --> 00:10:55,056
brief which measurement would confirm it
and what you predict that measurement will

153
00:10:55,096 --> 00:10:59,336
show. Then gather the evidence and compare.
Work alone, and write the document

154
00:10:59,376 --> 00:11:03,836
before any discussion. The answer file
holds the answers to the evidence questions

155
00:11:03,876 --> 00:11:08,390
and notes for the assessor. Don't read the
assessor notes before you have submitted

156
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:09,049
your design.

157
00:11:09,389 --> 00:11:15,589
The challenge is Exercise 34.5,
Level 3, "The design review", in

158
00:11:15,629 --> 00:11:18,514
exercises/m32-m34-practice.md.

159
00:11:22,354 --> 00:11:26,732
Answer out loud, in about five minutes.
A strong answer starts by placing the

160
00:11:26,772 --> 00:11:31,265
company in the decision table: it's in
several rows at once, and you name them.

161
00:11:31,305 --> 00:11:36,085
It then derives the long-lived refs from
the number of live versions and the release

162
00:11:36,125 --> 00:11:40,790
rhythm of each product, states one fix
direction with the invariant that tests it,

163
00:11:40,830 --> 00:11:45,323
and gives the rules per ref pattern with
their enforcing layer and their bypass

164
00:11:45,363 --> 00:11:45,610
path.

165
00:11:45,950 --> 00:11:50,532
For the auditor, it explains why a name
isn't an answer and what you show instead:

166
00:11:50,572 --> 00:11:54,759
where compliance comes from. That's the
auditor from the opening, answered.

167
00:11:54,799 --> 00:11:58,367
It claims nothing about research
that the research doesn't show.

168
00:12:00,207 --> 00:12:01,134
Let's land this.

169
00:12:01,474 --> 00:12:06,969
Level 8 ends with a design review: evidence,
branching and releases, governance, CI,

170
00:12:07,009 --> 00:12:09,407
security, reproducibility, migration.

171
00:12:09,747 --> 00:12:13,760
Evidence is gathered with read-only
commands, and each measurement is tied to a

172
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:15,813
statement of the brief, with its limits.

173
00:12:16,153 --> 00:12:21,204
Every decision has a reason, a cost and an
enforcing layer; a hook or a convention

174
00:12:21,244 --> 00:12:22,633
alone is not a control.

175
00:12:22,973 --> 00:12:27,459
In the defence you may defend, amend or
concede; an amendment has to say what it

176
00:12:27,499 --> 00:12:27,855
breaks.

177
00:12:28,195 --> 00:12:33,252
Never claim that research proves a strategy
best, never invent a feature, and never

178
00:12:33,292 --> 00:12:36,261
present a deletion commit
as removal of a secret.

179
00:12:38,101 --> 00:12:43,331
Write the design for Lab 34.1 in full, at
most four pages plus the evidence table.

180
00:12:43,371 --> 00:12:48,666
Then have a colleague or the tutor raise
three objections in strict interview mode:

181
00:12:48,706 --> 00:12:51,944
they ask, you answer, no
hints before your attempt.

182
00:12:51,984 --> 00:12:57,215
Record the objections you couldn't answer.
They're your revision list for Level 9.

183
00:12:57,255 --> 00:13:02,164
Finally, read reference/command-safety.md
before Part 9 begins, because every

184
00:13:02,204 --> 00:13:07,242
incident drill is scored on the safety
of the path as well as on the end state.

185
00:13:07,582 --> 00:13:11,976
You can now give every decision a
reason, a cost and an enforcing layer.

186
00:13:12,016 --> 00:13:16,902
Practise that in the lab. Next time: the
diagnosis method, and the push that had

187
00:13:16,942 --> 00:13:21,152
nothing to push. Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

188
00:13:21,192 --> 00:13:22,630
See you in the next one.
